.TH CHART 1 "August 2026" "chart" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
chart \- generate and read calibration charts and step wedges, and apply curves to images
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chart
.BR --generate " " OUT_IMAGE
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B chart
.I IMAGE
.I OUT_CSV
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B chart
.BR --step-wedge " " OUT_IMAGE
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B chart
.I IMAGE
.BR --read-wedge " " OUT_CSV
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B chart
.I IMAGE
.BR --apply " " CURVE " " OUTPUT
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B chart
is a command-line tool (Python script) that supports four workflows:
.IP 1. 3
Generate a printable
.IR N x N
grayscale ramp chart image (with a thick outer border and internal grid lines).
.IP 2. 3
Read a photographed/scanned chart image, detect/warp the chart grid, measure each patch,
normalize measurements, and emit CSV suitable for curve fitting (e.g. with the companion
.BR acv
tool).
.IP 3. 3
Generate a step wedge, or read one back from a photograph of a print.
.IP 4. 3
Apply an
.BR .acv ", " .amp " or " .csv
curve to an image, optionally appending a step wedge to it.
.PP
The generator can optionally permute (shuffle) the patch levels using several spatial
permutations intended to reduce adjacency bias in capture/printing.
.SH MODES
Mode is selected by which of the mode options is present. If more than one is
given, the first match in this order wins:
.BR --apply ", " --read-wedge ", " --generate ", " --step-wedge ,
otherwise reader mode.
.SS Generator mode
Generator mode is enabled with
.BR --generate .
It writes an image with an outer black border, internal grid lines, and filled grayscale patches.
.SS Reader mode
Reader mode is enabled when no mode option is given, and takes two positional
arguments,
.I IMAGE
and
.IR OUT_CSV .
It reads an input image, attempts to detect the outer border quadrilateral,
warps to a square canvas, measures per-cell median values, normalizes them to 0..255, and writes CSV.
.SS Step wedge mode
.B --step-wedge
on its own writes a step wedge image to the first positional argument. The wedge
is a vertical column of
.B --segments
patches (21 by default) spaced according to
.BR --progression ,
each numbered and labelled with its 8-bit value and optical density. Beside it
are a continuous gradient strip and a column of white/black bars: one white
segment at the top, one at the bottom, black between. Those bars are what
.B --read-wedge
looks for, so leave them in place when printing.
.PP
.B --step-wedge
combined with
.B --apply
does not write a wedge on its own; it appends one to the right of the image being
processed, with the same curve applied to it.
.SS Read wedge mode
.B --read-wedge
.I OUT_CSV
reads a photograph or scan of a printed wedge. It looks for the right-hand edge of
the white/black bar column in the rightmost 30% of the image, derives the wedge
geometry from the bar height, corrects perspective, and measures the median of
each segment. If the bars cannot be found it falls back to measuring the
rightmost strip of the image, which is only useful for a squarely-cropped scan.
The CSV holds the expected value of each segment against the measured one.
.SS Apply mode
.B --apply
.I CURVE OUTPUT
applies a curve to the image named by the first positional argument.
.B chart
reads
.BR .acv ", " .amp " and " .csv
curves itself, so
.B acv
is not needed for this. The curve becomes a lookup table at the bit depth of the
image, 8- or 16-bit. A single-channel curve is applied to every channel; a
multi-channel curve is applied one channel at a time.
.PP
The image is rotated to portrait if it is landscape, padded with white by
.BR --margin ,
and resized by
.B --size
(area averaging when shrinking, Lanczos when enlarging, leaving room for the wedge
if one is being appended). Output is written as grayscale, and as 16-bit if
.I OUTPUT
ends with
.BR .tif .
.SH OUTPUT
.SS Reader CSV format
The reader writes a CSV with header:
.PP
.nf
channel,x,y
.fi
.PP
Where:
.IP \[bu] 3
.B channel
is always 0.
.IP \[bu] 3
.B x
is the intended (printed) ramp value per patch (0..255), taking the selected
.BR --shuffle
method into account.
.IP \[bu] 3
.B y
is the measured patch value normalized so the darkest measured patch maps to 0
and the lightest maps to 255.
.PP
Notes:
.IP \[bu] 3
For correct interpretation,
.BR --shuffle
in reader mode must match the shuffle method used to generate/print the chart.
.IP \[bu] 3
The current implementation does not have a reader-side
.BR --invert-chart
flag; if you generated an inverted chart, you will need to invert the intended axis
in a post-processing step.
.SH OPTIONS
The options below the first group are tuning parameters. They work, but they are
hidden from
.B --help
because the defaults are almost always what you want.
.TP
.BR --generate " " OUT_IMAGE
Generate a chart image at
.IR OUT_IMAGE .
The output format is inferred from the filename extension (e.g. .png, .jpg, .tif).
If
.IR OUT_IMAGE
ends with
.BR .tif ,
the file is written as 16-bit (8-bit values scaled by 257).
.TP
.BR --step-wedge
Generate a step wedge to the first positional argument, or append one to the
image when used with
.BR --apply .
.TP
.BR --read-wedge " " OUT_CSV
Read a printed step wedge from the image named by the first positional argument
and write expected/measured pairs to
.IR OUT_CSV .
.TP
.BR --apply " " CURVE " " OUTPUT
Apply
.IR CURVE
.RB ( .acv ", " .amp " or " .csv )
to the image named by the first positional argument and write
.IR OUTPUT .
.TP
.BR --size " " PX
Apply: resize the image so its longest dimension is
.IR PX
pixels, before the curve is applied. No default; the image is left at its
original size if this is not given.
.TP
.BR --progression " " METHOD
Step wedge spacing, one of
.BR linear ", " stops " or " inv_stops .
Default:
.BR linear .
See
.B STEP WEDGE PROGRESSIONS
below.
.TP
.BR --grid " " N
Chart/Reader grid size
.IR N
for an
.IR N x N
chart. Default: 32.
.TP
.BR --shuffle " " METHOD
Select the shuffle/permutation method. Default:
.BR blue_noise .
See
.B SHUFFLE METHODS
below.
.TP
.BR --invert-chart
Generator: invert patch levels (0 <-> 255) before shuffling. Default: off.
.TP
.BR --cell " " PX
Generator: cell size in pixels (patch size). Default: 40. Hidden.
.TP
.BR --margin " " PX
Generator: outer margin in pixels (white area around grid). Default: 40.
In apply mode this is the white padding added around the image before it is
resized. Hidden.
.TP
.BR --line " " PX
Generator: internal grid line thickness in pixels. Default: 1. Hidden.
.TP
.BR --border " " PX
Generator: outer border thickness in pixels (black). Default: 10. Hidden.
.TP
.BR --segments " " N
Step wedge: number of segments. Default: 21. This must match between generating
a wedge and reading it back.
.B --read-wedge
derives the whole wedge geometry from it \(em the detected bar column spans
.IR segments-2
segments, and the warped wedge is divided into
.IR segments
bands to measure \(em so a mismatch misplaces both the crop and every
measurement. Hidden.
.TP
.BR --warpsize " " PX
Reader: warped canvas size (pixels) used after perspective correction.
Default: 1000. Hidden. In v1.0 this option was called
.BR --size ,
which now means something else \(em see
.B COMPATIBILITY
below.
.TP
.BR --debug " " PATH
Reader: save the warped grayscale image to
.IR PATH
(for inspection/debug). In read wedge mode it also writes the detected vertical
lines to a
.I _lines
variant of the same path. Hidden.
.TP
.BR --variance " " OUT_IMAGE
Reader: after reading the chart, write a variance chart image to
.IR OUT_IMAGE
and a matching CSV alongside it. Each patch shows how far its measurement sits
from the mean of the other patches printed at the same level, as a fraction of
that mean, offset so that no deviation is mid grey. This visualizes spatial
falloff/illumination variance when there are multiple samples per level. Hidden.
.SH STEP WEDGE PROGRESSIONS
.TP
.B linear
Patch values step evenly from 255 down to 0.
.TP
.B stops
Patch values halve every third segment, i.e. one third of a stop per segment,
starting at 255.
.TP
.B inv_stops
The complement of
.BR stops :
each patch is 255 minus the value it would have had, while the density printed on
the label is still that of the uncomplemented value.
.SH SHUFFLE METHODS
These methods permute the association between patch location (row-major position)
and patch value:
.TP
.B none
No permutation; patches are filled in row-major 0..255 order.
.TP
.B blue_noise
Deterministic blue-noise / void-and-cluster style ordering designed to make adjacent
patches differ strongly in value on average.
.TP
.B hilbert
Hilbert-curve based ordering.
.TP
.B split
Recursive even/odd splitting style permutation.
.TP
.B shuffle
Random shuffle (deterministic seed).
.TP
.B maximize_closest
Iterative local swapping heuristic to maximize neighborhood value separation.
.TP
.B gcd
Stride-based permutation using a stride chosen to maximize Manhattan-distance statistics on the grid.
.SH NOTES
.IP \[bu] 3
Reader mode attempts to locate the outer chart border using edge detection and contour approximation.
If it cannot find a suitable quadrilateral, it falls back to using the full image (resize-only).
.IP \[bu] 3
Per-patch measurement is a median over a centered ROI covering roughly 2/3 of the cell
to reduce contamination from grid lines.
.IP \[bu] 3
Normalization is performed on measured medians so the darkest measured patch becomes 0 and
the lightest becomes 255. This makes output robust to exposure differences, but it does not
recover absolute reflectance/irradiance.
.IP \[bu] 3
Step wedge measurements are not normalized. The CSV holds raw medians against
the expected values, so a wedge read back tells you about absolute placement in
a way a chart does not.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Charts are not interchangeable between versions of
.BR chart .
The
.B blue_noise
ordering changed after v1.0, so a chart printed with v1.0 and read with a later
version has every patch attributed to the wrong intended level, silently. Generate
and read a chart with the same version, or pass the same
.B --shuffle
method other than
.B blue_noise
at both ends.
.PP
.B --size
also changed meaning after v1.0. It used to be the reader's warped canvas size and
is now the apply-mode output size; the reader option is
.BR --warpsize .
A v1.0 reader command line using
.B --size
is still accepted but the option now has no effect there.
.PP
.B --margin
defaulted to 80 in v1.0 and now defaults to 40, so generated charts are 80 pixels
smaller in each dimension than they used to be.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.B chart
requires:
.IP \[bu] 3
opencv-python (cv2)
.IP \[bu] 3
numpy
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# Generate a 16x16 printable chart (PNG)
chart --generate chart.png --grid 16 --cell 40 --margin 80 --border 10 --shuffle blue_noise

# Generate a 32x32 chart (useful for multiple samples per level)
chart --generate chart32.tif --grid 32 --cell 30 --margin 80 --border 10 --shuffle blue_noise

# Read a photographed chart and write CSV
chart photo.jpg measurements.csv --grid 16 --shuffle blue_noise

# Read and also save a debug warped image
chart photo.jpg measurements.csv --grid 16 --shuffle blue_noise --debug warped.png

# Read and generate a variance visualization
chart photo.jpg measurements.csv --grid 32 --shuffle blue_noise --variance variance.png

# Generate a 21-segment step wedge
chart --step-wedge wedge.tif

# Generate a step wedge in third-stop steps
chart --step-wedge wedge.tif --progression stops

# Apply a correction curve to an image
chart photo.tif --apply correction.acv photo-corrected.tif

# Apply a curve, resize to 2000px, and append a corrected step wedge
chart photo.tif --apply correction.acv print.tif --size 2000 --step-wedge

# Read the step wedge back from a photograph of the print
chart print-photo.jpg --read-wedge wedge.csv
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR acv (1)
